{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BVRyxqJN2EPHcPpCiYp6P1","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a comprehensive view of the paper. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n# Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods Soundness and Experimental Rigor\n\n**Paper:** \"Structural Self-Consistency for Quality Estimation in LLM-Based Information Extraction\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. Methodological Soundness\n\n### 1.1 Signal Definitions and Fair Comparison\n\nThe five signals are clearly defined in Section 3 with explicit equations (Eq. 1--7). The key per-sample vs. instance-level distinction is well-motivated and becomes central to the correlation-selection gap analysis -- this is a genuine conceptual contribution.\n\nHowever, several concerns arise:\n\n- **Soft Jaccard (Eq. 4):** The numerator |M| counts matched extraction pairs from Hungarian matching, but the denominator uses max(|A_i|, |A_j|). This is non-standard -- classical Jaccard uses |A_i union A_j| = |A_i| + |A_j| - |intersection|, and the \"soft\" extension should clarify how span-overlap similarity feeds into the matching cost. The paper states \"span overlap as the similarity metric\" for bipartite matching but never defines the matching threshold or whether partial matches (e.g., overlap > 0.5) count as matched. This under-specification is a weakness: different thresholds could change SJ values materially. The decisions.yaml reveals the implementation uses \"type-grouped Hungarian matching with max(|g_a|,|g_b|) denominator\" -- this should be explicit in the paper.\n\n- **Entity Voting (Eq. 6-7):** The definition averages freq(e) over all entities in E_union, but the matching criterion for \"e in y_i\" is unspecified. Is this exact string match? Span overlap? If exact string, then voting is essentially a surface signal despite being classified as \"structural.\" The paper classifies it as structural because it operates on \"extracted entity/relation sets,\" but the actual matching mechanism matters for this classification.\n\n- **Selection strategy for instance-level signals:** Section 3.3 states that for best-of-N, instance-level signals \"select the sample closest to the consensus output, i.e., the one with highest average pairwise similarity to all other samples.\" This is a crucial design choice that introduces a confound: the selection performance of SJ/Voting/FK/EM is conflated with the quality of this auxiliary consensus-proximity heuristic. An alternative (e.g., picking the sample whose individual extraction set has highest overlap with the union/intersection of all samples) might perform differently. The paper should acknowledge this and ideally test at least one alternative.\n\n### 1.2 Problem Formulation Clarity\n\nThe quality estimation vs. sample selection decomposition is clean and well-argued. The paper correctly identifies the per-sample vs. instance-level distinction as the root cause of the correlation-selection gap. This framing is one of the paper's strongest contributions.\n\n### 1.3 Evaluation Metrics\n\n- **Spearman rho and Kendall tau:** Appropriate for rank correlation with continuous F1. No concerns.\n- **AUROC:** Defined as binary discrimination \"instances above vs. below median F1.\" This is reasonable but the choice of median as threshold is somewhat arbitrary. Sensitivity to this threshold is not discussed. For datasets with highly skewed F1 distributions (e.g., RE with 44% zero-F1), the median itself is a poor split point -- AUROC might look inflated because the \"easy\" discrimination is between zero-F1 and non-zero-F1 instances.\n- **Best-of-N F1:** Well-defined. The inclusion of oracle and greedy baselines is essential and well-executed. The random baseline is useful.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experimental Rigor\n\n### 2.1 Model-Dataset Matrix\n\nThe 2x2 matrix (Qwen x {SciERC, CoNLL} + LLaMA x {SciERC, CoNLL}) covers two models and two in-domain datasets. This is the minimum viable evidence for cross-model and cross-dataset claims. Concerns:\n\n- Both models are 8B parameters. The paper acknowledges this limitation, but the claim \"model-agnostic\" is arguably overstated for two same-scale models from different families.\n- Only one RE dataset (SciERC RE). The generalization of RE-specific claims (Claim 3 on compound error propagation) is limited to a single benchmark with a specific relation schema. The paper is honest about this.\n- WNUT-17 is used only as a negative case study. It serves Claim 3 well, but the paper's positive results rest on only two datasets.\n\n**Assessment:** Sufficient for an initial study; the cross-model Spearman delta of 0.005 on CoNLL is compelling. The 2x2 matrix is thin but defensible for EMNLP. The single-RE-dataset limitation is the biggest experimental gap.\n\n### 2.2 Sample Sizes\n\n- SciERC NER: n=529, SciERC RE: n=397, CoNLL: n=2756 (after filtering). These are adequate for the reported metrics. The CoNLL sample is large enough for tight confidence intervals.\n- SciERC RE n=397 with 44% zero-F1 means only ~220 instances contribute meaningful signal. This effective sample size is borderline for the number of comparisons made.\n\n### 2.3 Seed Robustness\n\nThe 3-seed analysis is performed on two configurations: Qwen x SciERC (NER, N=16) and LLaMA x CoNLL (NER, N=16). Results:\n\n- Qwen SciERC: SJ rho = 0.412 +/- 0.025 (CV=6%)\n- LLaMA CoNLL: SJ rho = 0.463 +/- 0.008 (CV<2%)\n\nThis is informative but incomplete. The 3-seed protocol covers only NER. RE has no multi-seed analysis in the main paper (the memory.md mentions 2-seed RE with delta = 0.002, but this is not in the paper). Given that RE results are more fragile, seed robustness for RE would strengthen the claims substantially.\n\n### 2.4 Temperature Analysis\n\nOnly T=0.7 and T=1.0. The paper reports bootstrap CIs include zero for SJ temperature differences, which is the correct statistical treatment. However:\n\n- Two temperature points cannot characterize the temperature-signal relationship. Is it monotonic? Does it plateau?\n- Log-probability is excluded from the temperature comparison (no T=0.7 logprob data). The paper acknowledges this in Limitations, but it weakens the comparative temperature analysis.\n- The claim that conditional filtering dominates temperature tuning cannot be fully evaluated without logprob temperature data.\n\n### 2.5 Sample Budget Scaling\n\nN=8 and N=16 are tested. The N=200 subset for RE N-scaling (mentioned in paper_framing.md) was later retracted after full-set replication. The final paper uses full-set N=16 results.\n\n- The N range is narrow. N=8 to N=16 is a single doubling. Whether gains plateau or continue is unknown.\n- SJ N-scaling gain is setting-dependent (Qwen SciERC +0.071, LLaMA SciERC +0.016), which the paper honestly reports. This variability undermines strong N-scaling claims.\n\n### 2.6 OOD Experiment\n\nSciERC -> WNUT-17 (one direction only). WNUT-17 is an OOD transfer where the model fine-tuned on SciERC is evaluated on social media text.\n\n- This is a single OOD direction. A reverse direction (CoNLL -> SciERC) or cross-domain validation on a third in-domain benchmark would strengthen boundary claims.\n- The WNUT-17 result is heavily confounded by model capability (54% zero-F1). It is difficult to separate \"OOD signal degradation\" from \"model incapable of the task.\" The paper correctly frames this as a capability boundary, not a signal limitation per se.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Statistical Concerns\n\n### 3.1 Confidence Intervals and Significance Tests\n\n- **Bootstrap CIs:** Mentioned for temperature comparisons (include zero) and N-scaling (SJ N-scaling is \"the only signal whose 95% CI excludes 0\" per memory.md). However, bootstrap CIs are not systematically reported in the main paper tables. Table 1 and Table 2 have no error bars or CIs. For a paper making comparative claims, the absence of CIs on the primary comparison table is a notable gap.\n\n- **Fisher z-test:** Used for NER vs. RE rho comparison (p=0.021). Appropriate.\n\n- **3-seed standard deviation:** Reported for Table 3 footnote (sigma=0.025). Adequate for the configurations tested.\n\n### 3.2 Multiple Comparisons\n\nThe paper makes many pairwise comparisons (5 signals x multiple settings x multiple metrics) without any multiple comparison correction. With approximately 20-30 implicit comparisons per table, some observed orderings could be chance variation. The CoNLL SJ vs. FK gap of 0.003-0.004 in particular is almost certainly within noise.\n\n### 3.3 Effect Size Reporting\n\nThe paper reports raw rho values and deltas, which is appropriate. However, confidence intervals on the deltas between signals (e.g., \"SJ rho - logprob rho = 0.217, 95% CI [...]\") would substantially strengthen the comparative claims. The memory.md mentions bootstrap CIs for some comparisons (CoNLL SJ-logprob: 95% CI [+0.088, +0.154]) but these do not appear in the paper.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Experiment Coverage\n\n### 4.1 Planned Experiments\n\n- **exp-011 (ner_ood_robustness):** SciERC -> WNUT-17 zero-shot. Covered in Section 4.3 (Method boundary: WNUT-17) and potentially Table 6 (Appendix). The expected results match: greedy F1 drops, structural signals weak, logprob negative. **Covered.**\n\n- **exp-017 (ner_llama_conll):** LLaMA x CoNLL completing the 2x2 matrix + 3-seed robustness. Table 1 includes CoNLL (LLaMA) row. Table 4 shows 3-seed results. **Covered.**\n\n### 4.2 Missing Experiments That Would Strengthen Claims\n\n1. **Supervised upper bound beyond logistic regression.** A simple fine-tuned verifier would quantify how much of the oracle gap is recoverable. Even a linear model trained on the five signals with cross-validation would be informative.\n\n2. **RE on a second dataset.** ACE05 or TACRED RE would establish whether the \"compound error\" claim generalizes or is SciERC-specific. This is the single biggest empirical gap.\n\n3. **N > 16.** A single experiment at N=32 or N=64 would clarify scaling behavior.\n\n4. **Closed-source API evaluation.** The discussion mentions consistency signals being useful when logprob is unavailable (closed-source APIs). Even one experiment with an API-based model (where only outputs, not logprobs, are available) would make this practical argument concrete.\n\n5. **Calibration analysis.** ECE is mentioned once (SJ achieves \"lowest calibration error, ECE=0.075-0.102\") but reliability diagrams are referenced as figures without being shown in the sections I reviewed. More thorough calibration analysis would be valuable given the \"logprob paradox\" finding.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Strengths and Weaknesses\n\n### Strengths\n\n- The quality estimation vs. sample selection decomposition is a genuine conceptual contribution that clarifies confusion in prior work. The \"correlation-selection gap\" framing is novel and well-argued.\n- The per-sample vs. instance-level signal distinction is crisp, correctly identifies the root cause of selection failure, and has practical implications.\n- Honesty in reporting negative results: NER selection failure, WNUT-17 boundary, RE weakness. The paper does not oversell.\n- The 2x2 model-dataset matrix with cross-model delta of 0.005 on CoNLL is strong model-agnostic evidence for structural signals.\n- The task-difficulty gradient (CoNLL -> SciERC -> WNUT-17) is well-designed and yields a coherent narrative.\n- The paper identifies the \"logprob paradox\" (high AUROC, low rho) and correctly interprets it as a distinction between binary filtering and continuous ranking.\n- Comprehensive robustness analyses: temperature, N-scaling, seed stability, LOO-SJ, conditional filtering, capability stratification.\n- Well-written experiments section with clear tables and transparent reporting.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Soft Jaccard under-specified.** The matching threshold and entity-matching criterion for voting are not explicitly defined. The discrepancy between the paper's simplified equation and the actual implementation (noted in decisions.yaml as a CRITICAL issue) is concerning.\n- **No confidence intervals on primary comparison tables.** Tables 1-2 lack error bars or bootstrap CIs for any signal comparison. Given the number of comparisons and the narrow gaps in some settings (CoNLL SJ vs. FK = 0.003), this is a significant omission.\n- **Single RE dataset.** Claim 3 (compound error propagation) rests entirely on SciERC RE. Generalization is unvalidated.\n- **Narrow N range (8-16).** Two points define a line, not a curve.\n- **Selection strategy confound.** The consensus-proximity heuristic used for instance-level signal selection is untested against alternatives. Selection failure might be partially attributable to this heuristic rather than the signals themselves.\n- **No multiple comparison correction.** Many pairwise signal comparisons without adjustment.\n- **Temperature analysis incomplete.** Only two temperatures; logprob excluded.\n- **Both models are 8B.** \"Model-agnostic\" is overstated; \"architecture-agnostic within the 8B scale\" is more accurate.\n- **The 2.3pp RE selection gain for logprob lacks significance testing.** Is +2.3pp over greedy on n=397 instances statistically significant? No CI or p-value is provided for this claim.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. What is the exact matching criterion used in entity voting confidence? Is an entity e considered present in sample y_i via exact string match or span overlap? If exact string, how does this differ in principle from a surface signal?\n\n2. For the best-of-N selection with instance-level signals, you select the sample closest to consensus. Did you test alternative selection strategies (e.g., selecting the sample with the median SJ score, or the sample closest to the mode)? How sensitive is the selection failure result to this heuristic?\n\n3. Can you provide bootstrap confidence intervals for the key signal-vs-signal deltas in Table 1 (e.g., Voting rho - logprob rho, SJ rho - logprob rho)? The memory.md mentions these exist for some comparisons but they are not in the paper.\n\n4. The +2.3pp RE selection gain for logprob: is this statistically significant? What is the bootstrap 95% CI on the F1 difference (logprob selection F1 - greedy F1)?\n\n5. The SJ equation (Eq. 4) uses |M| / max(|A_i|, |A_j|). But the implementation uses type-grouped Hungarian matching with soft similarity scores. Is |M| the count of matches above some threshold, or the sum of similarity scores? This distinction materially affects SJ values.\n\n6. At N=16, logprob achieves rho=0.287 on RE full set while SJ achieves 0.281 -- logprob actually overtakes SJ. Does this not weaken the \"structural signals dominate\" narrative for RE?\n\n7. The conditional filtering removes ~44% of RE instances (zero-F1). How sensitive are conditional results to the filtering threshold? Would filtering at F1 < 0.1 or F1 < 0.2 change the signal rankings?\n\n8. Why was logprob excluded from the temperature comparison? Collecting T=0.7 logprob data requires no additional model runs -- it is an output of the same inference call that produces the samples.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Preliminary Score\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper makes a clear conceptual contribution in the quality estimation vs. sample selection decomposition and the identification of the correlation-selection gap. The experimental design is systematic, the reporting is honest (including negative results), and the 2x2 model-dataset matrix provides reasonable evidence for the main claims. The writing quality is high, particularly in the experiments section.\n\nHowever, the paper has meaningful methodological gaps: under-specified SJ implementation, missing confidence intervals on primary tables, a single RE dataset for compound-error claims, and the untested selection heuristic confound. The 8B-only model evaluation limits \"model-agnostic\" claims. The narrow N and temperature ranges leave important scaling questions unanswered.\n\nThe work is above the rejection threshold because: (1) the conceptual contribution (correlation-selection gap) is novel and practically important, (2) the empirical evidence, while not exhaustive, is systematically collected and honestly reported, and (3) the paper identifies genuine boundary conditions rather than overclaiming. It falls short of a confident accept because the methodological under-specifications (SJ definition, selection heuristic, missing CIs) leave room for the quantitative claims to shift under scrutiny, and the single-RE-dataset limitation weakens generalization.\n\nWith confidence intervals added to Tables 1-2, a clearer SJ specification, and acknowledgment of the selection heuristic confound, this would be a solid 5. As submitted, it is a borderline paper leaning positive."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a3ac576549123d840 (use SendMessage with to: 'a3ac576549123d840' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 109562\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 155528</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5f10f0e-4112-4acf-bcc8-7fa2b85fb642","uuid":"e164b0c0-b980-4f05-9ce9-b370ef0c3b3e","timestamp":"2026-05-14T21:21:20.314Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Methods soundness and Experimental rigor**.\n\nReview standard: top ML venue, acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Structural Self-Consistency for Quality Estimation in LLM-Based Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper systematically compares 5 unsupervised confidence signals for quality estimation and sample selection in LLM-based structured IE:\n- **Signals:** log-probability (logprob), exact match (EM), Fleiss' κ (FK), Soft Jaccard (SJ), entity voting confidence (Vote)\n- **Tasks:** NER and RE\n- **Models:** Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B (both LoRA fine-tuned)\n- **Datasets:** SciERC, CoNLL-2003 (in-domain); WNUT-2017 (OOD appendix)\n- **Evaluation:** Quality estimation (Spearman ρ, Kendall τ, AUROC) and best-of-N selection (F1)\n\n**3 Main Claims:**\n1. Structural consistency signals (voting, SJ) are strongest unsupervised quality estimators for IE, outperforming logprob by 17–30 AUROC points and 0.2–0.4 in ρ.\n2. A \"correlation–selection gap\" exists: strong instance-level quality prediction doesn't translate to effective best-of-N selection on NER; only logprob enables selection on RE (+2.4pp).\n3. Task-complexity boundary: all signals degrade on RE due to compound error propagation; conditional filtering restores discriminative power.\n\n## Key Experimental Details\n\n**Table 1 (SciERC, Qwen3-8B, N=8):**\n- NER (n=529): Vote ρ=0.502/AUROC=0.728; SJ ρ=0.357/AUROC=0.666; logprob ρ=0.140/AUROC=0.559\n- RE (n=397): Vote ρ=0.422/AUROC=0.716; FK ρ=0.253/AUROC=0.631; logprob ρ=0.247/AUROC=0.635\n\n**Table 2 (2×2 matrix, Spearman ρ, full set, N=8):**\n- All 4 configs: structural > logprob (100% rate)\n- Cross-model Δρ ≤ 0.006 on same dataset\n- Qwen×SciERC: SJ 0.357, Vote 0.502, logprob 0.140\n- Qwen×CoNLL: SJ 0.436, Vote 0.424, logprob 0.316\n- LLaMA×SciERC: SJ 0.363, Vote —, logprob 0.183\n- LLaMA×CoNLL: SJ 0.431, Vote 0.426, logprob 0.307\n\n**Table 3 (AUROC, 2×2 matrix):**\n- 3/4 configs structural leads; exception: LLaMA×CoNLL logprob 0.761 > SJ 0.704\n- Paper explains: high-baseline regime (greedy F1=0.927), logprob well-calibrated for thresholding but ρ still inferior\n\n**Table 4 (3-seed robustness, LLaMA×CoNLL, N=16):**\n- SJ ρ = 0.463±0.008, voting ρ = 0.458±0.003, logprob ρ = 0.310±0.005\n- structural beats logprob rate = 100%\n\n**Table 5 (Best-of-N selection, SciERC):**\n- NER: Oracle 0.769, Greedy 0.609, all signals ≤ 0.609 (no signal beats greedy!)\n- RE: Oracle 0.561, Greedy 0.374, logprob 0.397 (+2.4pp), structural signals ≤ 0.368\n\n**Table 6 (OOD: SciERC→WNUT-2017, Qwen3-8B):**\n- Full set: all ρ near zero; logprob turns negative (−0.031)\n- Conditional: SJ 0.198, Vote 0.224, logprob 0.117\n- Relative signal ordering preserved\n\n**Additional analyses:** Temperature (Δρ ≤ ±0.01 for SJ), ensemble (logistic regression marginal gains), sample budget scaling (n=200 subset, N=8→16 improves RE signals)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe imagined experiment plan includes:\n1. **exp-011 (ner_ood_robustness):** SciERC→WNUT-17 zero-shot. Expected: greedy F1 drops to 0.143, structural signals weak but positive, logprob goes negative. Paper covers this in Appendix A / Table 6. ✓\n2. **exp-017 (ner_llama_conll):** LLaMA×CoNLL completing the 2×2 matrix + 3-seed robustness. Expected: SJ ρ=0.431, cross-model Δ=0.005. Paper covers in Tables 2, 3, 4. ✓\n\n## Your Review Tasks\n\nPlease provide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**\n   - Are the 5 signals well-defined and fairly compared?\n   - Is the problem formulation (quality estimation vs. sample selection) clean?\n   - Any concerns about the evaluation metrics (ρ, τ, AUROC, best-of-N F1)?\n   - Is the Soft Jaccard metric well-motivated and correctly implemented?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor**\n   - Is the 2×2 model-dataset matrix sufficient for generalization claims?\n   - Are the sample sizes (n=529, 397, 2756) adequate?\n   - Is the 3-seed robustness analysis on only one configuration (LLaMA×CoNLL) sufficient?\n   - Temperature analysis: only two temperatures (0.7, 1.0) — is this enough?\n   - Sample budget scaling: only n=200 subset — is this a limitation?\n   - OOD experiment: only one direction (SciERC→WNUT) — sufficient?\n\n3. **Statistical Concerns**\n   - Are confidence intervals / significance tests provided?\n   - Bootstrap CIs mentioned?\n   - Multiple comparison corrections?\n\n4. **Experiment Coverage**\n   - Does the paper cover the planned experiments (exp-011, exp-017)?\n   - Any missing experiments that would strengthen claims?\n\n5. **Strengths and Weaknesses** (bulleted)\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n\n7. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 scale with justification)\n\nFormat your review as a structured document with clear headers. Be rigorous but fair.","agentId":"a3ac576549123d840","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a comprehensive view of the paper. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n# Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods Soundness and Experimental Rigor\n\n**Paper:** \"Structural Self-Consistency for Quality Estimation in LLM-Based Information Extraction\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. Methodological Soundness\n\n### 1.1 Signal Definitions and Fair Comparison\n\nThe five signals are clearly defined in Section 3 with explicit equations (Eq. 1--7). The key per-sample vs. instance-level distinction is well-motivated and becomes central to the correlation-selection gap analysis -- this is a genuine conceptual contribution.\n\nHowever, several concerns arise:\n\n- **Soft Jaccard (Eq. 4):** The numerator |M| counts matched extraction pairs from Hungarian matching, but the denominator uses max(|A_i|, |A_j|). This is non-standard -- classical Jaccard uses |A_i union A_j| = |A_i| + |A_j| - |intersection|, and the \"soft\" extension should clarify how span-overlap similarity feeds into the matching cost. The paper states \"span overlap as the similarity metric\" for bipartite matching but never defines the matching threshold or whether partial matches (e.g., overlap > 0.5) count as matched. This under-specification is a weakness: different thresholds could change SJ values materially. The decisions.yaml reveals the implementation uses \"type-grouped Hungarian matching with max(|g_a|,|g_b|) denominator\" -- this should be explicit in the paper.\n\n- **Entity Voting (Eq. 6-7):** The definition averages freq(e) over all entities in E_union, but the matching criterion for \"e in y_i\" is unspecified. Is this exact string match? Span overlap? If exact string, then voting is essentially a surface signal despite being classified as \"structural.\" The paper classifies it as structural because it operates on \"extracted entity/relation sets,\" but the actual matching mechanism matters for this classification.\n\n- **Selection strategy for instance-level signals:** Section 3.3 states that for best-of-N, instance-level signals \"select the sample closest to the consensus output, i.e., the one with highest average pairwise similarity to all other samples.\" This is a crucial design choice that introduces a confound: the selection performance of SJ/Voting/FK/EM is conflated with the quality of this auxiliary consensus-proximity heuristic. An alternative (e.g., picking the sample whose individual extraction set has highest overlap with the union/intersection of all samples) might perform differently. The paper should acknowledge this and ideally test at least one alternative.\n\n### 1.2 Problem Formulation Clarity\n\nThe quality estimation vs. sample selection decomposition is clean and well-argued. The paper correctly identifies the per-sample vs. instance-level distinction as the root cause of the correlation-selection gap. This framing is one of the paper's strongest contributions.\n\n### 1.3 Evaluation Metrics\n\n- **Spearman rho and Kendall tau:** Appropriate for rank correlation with continuous F1. No concerns.\n- **AUROC:** Defined as binary discrimination \"instances above vs. below median F1.\" This is reasonable but the choice of median as threshold is somewhat arbitrary. Sensitivity to this threshold is not discussed. For datasets with highly skewed F1 distributions (e.g., RE with 44% zero-F1), the median itself is a poor split point -- AUROC might look inflated because the \"easy\" discrimination is between zero-F1 and non-zero-F1 instances.\n- **Best-of-N F1:** Well-defined. The inclusion of oracle and greedy baselines is essential and well-executed. The random baseline is useful.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experimental Rigor\n\n### 2.1 Model-Dataset Matrix\n\nThe 2x2 matrix (Qwen x {SciERC, CoNLL} + LLaMA x {SciERC, CoNLL}) covers two models and two in-domain datasets. This is the minimum viable evidence for cross-model and cross-dataset claims. Concerns:\n\n- Both models are 8B parameters. The paper acknowledges this limitation, but the claim \"model-agnostic\" is arguably overstated for two same-scale models from different families.\n- Only one RE dataset (SciERC RE). The generalization of RE-specific claims (Claim 3 on compound error propagation) is limited to a single benchmark with a specific relation schema. The paper is honest about this.\n- WNUT-17 is used only as a negative case study. It serves Claim 3 well, but the paper's positive results rest on only two datasets.\n\n**Assessment:** Sufficient for an initial study; the cross-model Spearman delta of 0.005 on CoNLL is compelling. The 2x2 matrix is thin but defensible for EMNLP. The single-RE-dataset limitation is the biggest experimental gap.\n\n### 2.2 Sample Sizes\n\n- SciERC NER: n=529, SciERC RE: n=397, CoNLL: n=2756 (after filtering). These are adequate for the reported metrics. The CoNLL sample is large enough for tight confidence intervals.\n- SciERC RE n=397 with 44% zero-F1 means only ~220 instances contribute meaningful signal. This effective sample size is borderline for the number of comparisons made.\n\n### 2.3 Seed Robustness\n\nThe 3-seed analysis is performed on two configurations: Qwen x SciERC (NER, N=16) and LLaMA x CoNLL (NER, N=16). Results:\n\n- Qwen SciERC: SJ rho = 0.412 +/- 0.025 (CV=6%)\n- LLaMA CoNLL: SJ rho = 0.463 +/- 0.008 (CV<2%)\n\nThis is informative but incomplete. The 3-seed protocol covers only NER. RE has no multi-seed analysis in the main paper (the memory.md mentions 2-seed RE with delta = 0.002, but this is not in the paper). Given that RE results are more fragile, seed robustness for RE would strengthen the claims substantially.\n\n### 2.4 Temperature Analysis\n\nOnly T=0.7 and T=1.0. The paper reports bootstrap CIs include zero for SJ temperature differences, which is the correct statistical treatment. However:\n\n- Two temperature points cannot characterize the temperature-signal relationship. Is it monotonic? Does it plateau?\n- Log-probability is excluded from the temperature comparison (no T=0.7 logprob data). The paper acknowledges this in Limitations, but it weakens the comparative temperature analysis.\n- The claim that conditional filtering dominates temperature tuning cannot be fully evaluated without logprob temperature data.\n\n### 2.5 Sample Budget Scaling\n\nN=8 and N=16 are tested. The N=200 subset for RE N-scaling (mentioned in paper_framing.md) was later retracted after full-set replication. The final paper uses full-set N=16 results.\n\n- The N range is narrow. N=8 to N=16 is a single doubling. Whether gains plateau or continue is unknown.\n- SJ N-scaling gain is setting-dependent (Qwen SciERC +0.071, LLaMA SciERC +0.016), which the paper honestly reports. This variability undermines strong N-scaling claims.\n\n### 2.6 OOD Experiment\n\nSciERC -> WNUT-17 (one direction only). WNUT-17 is an OOD transfer where the model fine-tuned on SciERC is evaluated on social media text.\n\n- This is a single OOD direction. A reverse direction (CoNLL -> SciERC) or cross-domain validation on a third in-domain benchmark would strengthen boundary claims.\n- The WNUT-17 result is heavily confounded by model capability (54% zero-F1). It is difficult to separate \"OOD signal degradation\" from \"model incapable of the task.\" The paper correctly frames this as a capability boundary, not a signal limitation per se.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Statistical Concerns\n\n### 3.1 Confidence Intervals and Significance Tests\n\n- **Bootstrap CIs:** Mentioned for temperature comparisons (include zero) and N-scaling (SJ N-scaling is \"the only signal whose 95% CI excludes 0\" per memory.md). However, bootstrap CIs are not systematically reported in the main paper tables. Table 1 and Table 2 have no error bars or CIs. For a paper making comparative claims, the absence of CIs on the primary comparison table is a notable gap.\n\n- **Fisher z-test:** Used for NER vs. RE rho comparison (p=0.021). Appropriate.\n\n- **3-seed standard deviation:** Reported for Table 3 footnote (sigma=0.025). Adequate for the configurations tested.\n\n### 3.2 Multiple Comparisons\n\nThe paper makes many pairwise comparisons (5 signals x multiple settings x multiple metrics) without any multiple comparison correction. With approximately 20-30 implicit comparisons per table, some observed orderings could be chance variation. The CoNLL SJ vs. FK gap of 0.003-0.004 in particular is almost certainly within noise.\n\n### 3.3 Effect Size Reporting\n\nThe paper reports raw rho values and deltas, which is appropriate. However, confidence intervals on the deltas between signals (e.g., \"SJ rho - logprob rho = 0.217, 95% CI [...]\") would substantially strengthen the comparative claims. The memory.md mentions bootstrap CIs for some comparisons (CoNLL SJ-logprob: 95% CI [+0.088, +0.154]) but these do not appear in the paper.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Experiment Coverage\n\n### 4.1 Planned Experiments\n\n- **exp-011 (ner_ood_robustness):** SciERC -> WNUT-17 zero-shot. Covered in Section 4.3 (Method boundary: WNUT-17) and potentially Table 6 (Appendix). The expected results match: greedy F1 drops, structural signals weak, logprob negative. **Covered.**\n\n- **exp-017 (ner_llama_conll):** LLaMA x CoNLL completing the 2x2 matrix + 3-seed robustness. Table 1 includes CoNLL (LLaMA) row. Table 4 shows 3-seed results. **Covered.**\n\n### 4.2 Missing Experiments That Would Strengthen Claims\n\n1. **Supervised upper bound beyond logistic regression.** A simple fine-tuned verifier would quantify how much of the oracle gap is recoverable. Even a linear model trained on the five signals with cross-validation would be informative.\n\n2. **RE on a second dataset.** ACE05 or TACRED RE would establish whether the \"compound error\" claim generalizes or is SciERC-specific. This is the single biggest empirical gap.\n\n3. **N > 16.** A single experiment at N=32 or N=64 would clarify scaling behavior.\n\n4. **Closed-source API evaluation.** The discussion mentions consistency signals being useful when logprob is unavailable (closed-source APIs). Even one experiment with an API-based model (where only outputs, not logprobs, are available) would make this practical argument concrete.\n\n5. **Calibration analysis.** ECE is mentioned once (SJ achieves \"lowest calibration error, ECE=0.075-0.102\") but reliability diagrams are referenced as figures without being shown in the sections I reviewed. More thorough calibration analysis would be valuable given the \"logprob paradox\" finding.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Strengths and Weaknesses\n\n### Strengths\n\n- The quality estimation vs. sample selection decomposition is a genuine conceptual contribution that clarifies confusion in prior work. The \"correlation-selection gap\" framing is novel and well-argued.\n- The per-sample vs. instance-level signal distinction is crisp, correctly identifies the root cause of selection failure, and has practical implications.\n- Honesty in reporting negative results: NER selection failure, WNUT-17 boundary, RE weakness. The paper does not oversell.\n- The 2x2 model-dataset matrix with cross-model delta of 0.005 on CoNLL is strong model-agnostic evidence for structural signals.\n- The task-difficulty gradient (CoNLL -> SciERC -> WNUT-17) is well-designed and yields a coherent narrative.\n- The paper identifies the \"logprob paradox\" (high AUROC, low rho) and correctly interprets it as a distinction between binary filtering and continuous ranking.\n- Comprehensive robustness analyses: temperature, N-scaling, seed stability, LOO-SJ, conditional filtering, capability stratification.\n- Well-written experiments section with clear tables and transparent reporting.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Soft Jaccard under-specified.** The matching threshold and entity-matching criterion for voting are not explicitly defined. The discrepancy between the paper's simplified equation and the actual implementation (noted in decisions.yaml as a CRITICAL issue) is concerning.\n- **No confidence intervals on primary comparison tables.** Tables 1-2 lack error bars or bootstrap CIs for any signal comparison. Given the number of comparisons and the narrow gaps in some settings (CoNLL SJ vs. FK = 0.003), this is a significant omission.\n- **Single RE dataset.** Claim 3 (compound error propagation) rests entirely on SciERC RE. Generalization is unvalidated.\n- **Narrow N range (8-16).** Two points define a line, not a curve.\n- **Selection strategy confound.** The consensus-proximity heuristic used for instance-level signal selection is untested against alternatives. Selection failure might be partially attributable to this heuristic rather than the signals themselves.\n- **No multiple comparison correction.** Many pairwise signal comparisons without adjustment.\n- **Temperature analysis incomplete.** Only two temperatures; logprob excluded.\n- **Both models are 8B.** \"Model-agnostic\" is overstated; \"architecture-agnostic within the 8B scale\" is more accurate.\n- **The 2.3pp RE selection gain for logprob lacks significance testing.** Is +2.3pp over greedy on n=397 instances statistically significant? No CI or p-value is provided for this claim.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. What is the exact matching criterion used in entity voting confidence? Is an entity e considered present in sample y_i via exact string match or span overlap? If exact string, how does this differ in principle from a surface signal?\n\n2. For the best-of-N selection with instance-level signals, you select the sample closest to consensus. Did you test alternative selection strategies (e.g., selecting the sample with the median SJ score, or the sample closest to the mode)? How sensitive is the selection failure result to this heuristic?\n\n3. Can you provide bootstrap confidence intervals for the key signal-vs-signal deltas in Table 1 (e.g., Voting rho - logprob rho, SJ rho - logprob rho)? The memory.md mentions these exist for some comparisons but they are not in the paper.\n\n4. The +2.3pp RE selection gain for logprob: is this statistically significant? What is the bootstrap 95% CI on the F1 difference (logprob selection F1 - greedy F1)?\n\n5. The SJ equation (Eq. 4) uses |M| / max(|A_i|, |A_j|). But the implementation uses type-grouped Hungarian matching with soft similarity scores. Is |M| the count of matches above some threshold, or the sum of similarity scores? This distinction materially affects SJ values.\n\n6. At N=16, logprob achieves rho=0.287 on RE full set while SJ achieves 0.281 -- logprob actually overtakes SJ. Does this not weaken the \"structural signals dominate\" narrative for RE?\n\n7. The conditional filtering removes ~44% of RE instances (zero-F1). How sensitive are conditional results to the filtering threshold? Would filtering at F1 < 0.1 or F1 < 0.2 change the signal rankings?\n\n8. Why was logprob excluded from the temperature comparison? Collecting T=0.7 logprob data requires no additional model runs -- it is an output of the same inference call that produces the samples.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Preliminary Score\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper makes a clear conceptual contribution in the quality estimation vs. sample selection decomposition and the identification of the correlation-selection gap. The experimental design is systematic, the reporting is honest (including negative results), and the 2x2 model-dataset matrix provides reasonable evidence for the main claims. The writing quality is high, particularly in the experiments section.\n\nHowever, the paper has meaningful methodological gaps: under-specified SJ implementation, missing confidence intervals on primary tables, a single RE dataset for compound-error claims, and the untested selection heuristic confound. The 8B-only model evaluation limits \"model-agnostic\" claims. The narrow N and temperature ranges leave important scaling questions unanswered.\n\nThe work is above the rejection threshold because: (1) the conceptual contribution (correlation-selection gap) is novel and practically important, (2) the empirical evidence, while not exhaustive, is systematically collected and honestly reported, and (3) the paper identifies genuine boundary conditions rather than overclaiming. It falls short of a confident accept because the methodological under-specifications (SJ definition, selection heuristic, missing CIs) leave room for the quantitative claims to shift under scrutiny, and the single-RE-dataset limitation weakens generalization.\n\nWith confidence intervals added to Tables 1-2, a clearer SJ specification, and acknowledgment of the selection heuristic confound, this would be a solid 5. As submitted, it is a borderline paper leaning positive."}],"totalDurationMs":155528,"totalTokens":109562,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4122,"cache_read_input_tokens":101167,"output_tokens":4272,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4122},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":4272,"cache_read_input_tokens":101167,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4122,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4122,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":12}}}
